Subregion name: 'defender_base'
Subregion description: Defender home base carved into the western canyon headwall: a fortified spawn pole of shipping containers, sandbag walls, scaffolding and supply crates with a faction banner, anchoring one end of the map with spawn protection and a readable team identity.
Subregion plan: This region is the western team base: a fortified spawn pole carved into the canyon headwall (canyon_wall_west bounds its back edge) that anchors one pole of the map and feeds outward into all three lanes. It is a long, tall slab of space (about 17.5m deep east-to-west, 47m wide north-to-south, rising the full canyon height). Organize it in three west-to-east bands. The rearmost (western) band, set against the carved cliff cut, is the protected spawn: a recessed excavation niche in the rock where the team materializes, kept safe from direct lane sightlines, marked clearly with the defending faction's large banner and stocked with supply crates and resupply materiel that establish team ownership. The middle band is the staging and fortification line: stacked shipping containers and sandbag emplacements form a defensive wall with deliberate gaps, scaffolding and catwalks layered above the containers give defenders elevated firing positions and a way to move along the base laterally, and dust-covered crates, tarps, and a generator dress it as a dug-in field camp. The forward (eastern) band is the base mouth, which must read as three distinct exits aligned to the lanes it borders: a southern opening toward the right market/loading-yard lane, a central opening toward the excavated temple courtyard, and a northern opening toward the tight left alley lane, each framed by cover (container ends, sandbag stacks, half-walls) so the transition out of spawn has a natural defensive rhythm and a readable chokepoint. Lateral movement across the base behind the fortification line lets the team rotate between the three lane mouths without exposure. Maintain a near-mirror relationship with the attacker_base at the opposite pole. The whole region reads as weathered sandstone cut by an excavation, militarized with industrial container-and-scaffold fortification and faction signage.

