        Plan for this region: This region is the central mid lane: the map's focal landmark, an excavated ancient-temple courtyard that runs the full 42m east-to-west length of the map between the two bases and is about 15.66m wide north-to-south, occupying the central band of the canyon floor. The defender base's central exit feeds in from the west and the attacker base's central exit feeds in from the east, so the courtyard is the long, open through-route down the spine of the map with the longest clear sightlines of any lane. Its north long edge borders the left_lane (separated by the run of ruined sandstone divider walls already standing there) and its south long edge borders the right_lane; both dividing edges must be left with deliberate breaches or gaps near the mid-map line so players can rotate laterally between mid and the two side lanes. Shape the space as a tiered excavation centered on a sunken plaza: the courtyard floor at and around the center dips below the surrounding canyon ground plane into a shallow rectangular pit reached by broad sunken stone steps descending from the higher approach ground on the west and east ends, so the center sits lower than the base mouths and reads as a partially dug-out temple foundation. At the dead center of this sunken plaza is the partially unearthed artifact, a large embedded stone relic half-exposed in the excavation, which serves as the contested control / bomb-plant objective; keep a clear plantable flat around it. Populate the plaza and its approaches with the wreckage of the buried temple — broken and toppled columns, standing column stubs, fallen capitals, cracked paving slabs and scattered carved blocks — arranged to give a steady rhythm of waist-to-chest-high cover that breaks the long east-west sightline into staggered firing angles without fully closing it, so the lane keeps its open long-range character while still being fightable. Layer in subtle verticality along the two long (north and south) edges: raised stone platforms or balconies set above the sunken plaza floor, reachable by ramps, broken stairs, or stepped rubble from the approach ground, giving elevated firing positions that overlook the artifact and the descending steps. Keep the whole region near-mirror-symmetric about the mid-map center line so the western (defender) approach and the eastern (attacker) approach into the pit match in cover spacing, step placement, and balcony layout, favoring neither side. Everything is weathered tan and rust-orange sandstone, dust-caked, sun-bleached, and battle-worn, consistent with the abandoned excavation dig-site language of the rest of the map, with loose grit and scattered rubble around the unearthed stonework.
